Insert Equation  into Equation , renaming the argument on the left $x'$: $\begin{aligned}
\psi(x')
&=
\int\frac{dp}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\,
e^{ipx'}
\left[
\int\frac{dx}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\,
\psi(x)e^{-ipx}
\right] \notag\\
&=
\int dx
\left[
\int\frac{dp}{2\pi}
e^{ip(x'-x)}
\right]\psi(x).
\end{aligned}$ The expression in square brackets must reproduce $\psi(x')$ for every suitable $\psi$. Comparing with the defining action of the delta gives $\boxed{
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\frac{dp}{2\pi}\,
e^{ip(x'-x)}
=\delta(x'-x)
}.$

